{"type":"data","nodes":[null,{"type":"data","data":[{"mission":1,"hero":514},{"id":2,"agency":3,"agency_full":4,"sector":5,"dest":6,"color":7,"year":8,"status":9,"departure_date":10,"arrival_date":11,"transit_days":12,"vehicle":13,"payload":14,"delta_v":15,"data_quality":16,"credit":17,"links":18,"flight_data_quality":27,"fleet_refs":28,"flight":34,"name":492,"type":493,"first":494,"description":495,"events":496,"vehicle_stage":36},"blue-moon-mk1","Blue Origin","Blue Origin Federation, LLC","private","MOON","#00264D",2027,"PLANNED","2027-06-01","2027-06-05",4,"New Glenn (1st stage reusable + 2nd stage expendable)","~3000 kg cargo to lunar surface (CLPS payloads)","~6 km/s","partial","© Blue Origin / NASA — Blue Moon Mark 1 cargo lunar lander (named Endurance), contracted under NASA's CLPS program. NET early 2027 following a New Glenn hotfire anomaly at LC-36 in May 2026. Single-stage BE-7-powered lander; refuelable architecture intended for the Mark 2 crewed variant, a candidate lander for Artemis IV and later landings.",[19,23],{"l":20,"u":21,"t":22},"Blue Moon — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Moon_(spacecraft)","intro",{"l":24,"u":25,"t":26},"NASA CLPS contract awards","https://www.nasa.gov/commercial-lunar-payload-services/","core","sparse",[29,32],{"id":30,"role":31},"new-glenn","launcher",{"id":2,"role":33},"spacecraft",{"launch":35,"cruise":39,"arrival":42,"totals":49,"events":53,"cislunar_profile":78},{"vehicle_stage":36,"departure":33,"mass_at_tli_kg":37,"source":38},"New Glenn 2nd stage (to low Earth orbit)",16000,"Blue Origin Blue Moon technical brief; New Glenn leaves MK1 in low Earth orbit and the lander's BE-7 performs TLI (Gizmodo, 2025-11-17, https://gizmodo.com/bezoss-big-rocket-has-proved-itself-heres-whats-next-for-new-glenn-2000686257)",{"tcm_count":40,"source":41},1,"Blue Moon Mark 1 mission profile (planned)",{"v_infinity_km_s":43,"periapsis_km":44,"inclination_deg":45,"orbit_insertion_dv_km_s":43,"source":46,"entry_velocity_km_s":47,"edl_system":48,"edl_duration_s":44,"touchdown_velocity_ms":40},0.85,100,null,"Blue Moon LOI burn parameters per Blue Origin published architecture (planned)",0.15,"powered",{"total_dv_km_s":50,"tli_or_tmi_dv_km_s":51,"source":52},6,3.1,"Blue Moon Mark 1 ∆v ledger (planned cargo descent)",[54,59,64,69,73],{"met_days":55,"type":56,"label":57,"description":58},0,"launch","Launch — New Glenn (planned)","Cape Canaveral LC-36, NET early 2027 (slipped from 2025 after a New Glenn hotfire anomaly in May 2026). Blue Origin's Blue Moon Mk1 cargo lander (named Endurance) — first uncrewed surface mission of Blue Origin's two-class lunar landing architecture. Mk1 carries ~3 metric tons of payload; the larger Mk2 is the human-rated lander NASA contracted for crewed Artemis landings.",{"met_days":60,"type":61,"label":62,"description":63},0.2,"tli_or_tmi","Trans-lunar injection","New Glenn leaves Mk1 in low Earth orbit; after three to four orbits the lander's own BE-7 raises it to an elliptical orbit, then burns for the Moon — a 4-day translunar coast.",{"met_days":65,"type":66,"label":67,"description":68},1.5,"tcm","MCC-1 — first midcourse correction","Refines the trajectory after TLI dispersion.",{"met_days":12,"type":70,"label":71,"description":72},"arrival","Lunar orbit insertion","Lander captures into a 100-km circular lunar orbit before descent.",{"met_days":74,"type":75,"label":76,"description":77},4.1,"edl_or_oi","Lunar powered descent — south polar region","Blue Moon's single BE-7 hydrogen/oxygen engine (the first hydrogen-fuelled lunar lander engine) executes the powered descent. Targets a south polar landing site near future Artemis crewed sites.",{"source_tier":79,"parking_orbit":80,"tli":83,"translunar":84,"lunar_arrival":86,"return":89,"waypoints_km":91},"tier_1_5_hybrid",{"altitude_km":81,"inclination_deg":82,"revs":40},200,28.5,{"dv_kms":51},{"type":85},"direct",{"type":87,"altitude_km":44,"inclination_deg":88,"periselene_km":44},"orbit_and_land",90,{"type":90}